++# Agent Note: The active profile's user patch layer is provided to the tree
++
++Status: implemented
++
++English | [中文](2026-10-12-profile-patch-service.zh.md)
++
++## Problem
++
++The launcher resolves the active profile's identity — name, directory, `cordis.patch.yml`, `patchReload` — in `composeProfile()` and watches that file with `watchUserPatches()`, but the tree sees only the launch environment and the app command line. A management surface that must persist a composition row therefore reconstructs `$DSH_HOME/profiles/<name>` from `DSH_HOME` and writes the launcher's own file: an MCP-server manager inserting `@deepseek-ai/dsh-mcp-client` instances, a skill manager, or a plugin inventory that wants to enable a row. Nothing in the row store carries the profile name, and `@deepseek-ai/dsh-home-paths` resolves only the harness home.
++
++Three failures follow from that reconstruction, each observed in the field. A row naming an unresolvable package exits the process during boot, so the check has to happen before the write, not after ([discussion #3473](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness/discussions/3473)). A write that lands during a reload can activate the replacement fiber while the previous scope is still alive ([discussion #8968](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness/discussions/8968)); that report is about entry re-activation ordering, and every writer of the file depends on that ordering being correct. Two writers race the launcher's watcher with no shared lock, and a plugin that mirrors the layer cannot learn that a generation landed. The documented user path stays manual: "merge the chosen file's single `insert` patch into a user patch layer — `$DSH_HOME/profiles/<name>/cordis.patch.yml` for one profile, or `$DSH_HOME/cordis.patch.yml` for every profile on the machine" ([mcp-memory guide](../../../docs/user/guide/mcp-memory.md)).
++
++## Decision
++
++`@deepseek-ai/dsh-app-boot` provides `ProfilePatches` on the host context, and the launcher provides it in `runProfile`'s boot `prepare` hook — before any config-tree entry mounts, beside `DSH_LAUNCH_ENVIRONMENT_KEY` and `provideCmdline`.
++
++`provideProfilePatches(ctx, { profile, homePatchPath, higherLayers })` takes the loaded `Profile` the launcher already holds, the home layer path, and a thunk returning the layers that outrank the profile layer (home layer, then `--patch` overlays). It returns the service plus an `applied(patches, filename)` hook.
++
++`ctx.profilePatches` carries the profile facts a tree cannot otherwise obtain — `name`, `dir`, `patchPath`, `homePatchPath`, `reload`:
++
++- `read()` returns the layer **as authored**. Relative plugin names stay relative, because `loadOptionalPatches()` anchors them to `file:` URLs at parse time and its output cannot be written back. A read-modify-write therefore round-trips `./probe.mjs` and `!!js process.env.…` config expressions unchanged.
++- `mutate(edit, { validate })` reads the layer, applies `edit`, runs `validate(next)`, and replaces the file with `writeFileAtomic` (mode `0o644`) inside `withFileLock` (`<patchPath>.lock`). A `validate` throw leaves the layer exactly as it was and releases the lock, so a writer that changes control-plane rows can refuse a candidate it cannot prove resolvable before the write that would break the next boot ([discussion #3473](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness/discussions/3473)). One lock-guarded read-modify-write is the whole write API; package installation, row semantics, and backup policy stay with the caller.
++- `subscribe(listener)` observes committed generations, and `watchUserPatches` accepts an `onApplied(userPatches, filename)` callback that fires after `entry.update()` settles. The service publishes only its own `patchPath`, so an external hand edit and another process are observed alike, and a `startup`-frozen profile never fires.
++- `higherPrecedenceIds()` returns the ids claimed by the home layer and the `--patch` overlays — explicit entry `id`s and inserted row `id`s — so a configuration surface refuses an edit the composed order would mask instead of persisting a dead row.
++
++`parseProfilePatches(binName, file, content)` is exported beside the service: it parses the same top-level array and loader dialect boot enforces, without anchoring, and fails loud on a non-array document or a non-mapping entry. The service's own reader treats an absent file as an empty layer, so a profile whose user layer was never authored still reads and writes.
++
++The watcher hook does not change reload semantics. `--dump-config` never provides the service, so a dump stays a read of the composition, and `reload` reports only whether a commit reaches the running tree without a restart.
++
++## Alternatives considered
++
++- **A launcher-local script or helper that the plugin runs.** The facts live in `apps/cli`, but a helper still has to reach the tree, and a tree-visible copy would be a second implementation of profile discovery — the duplication this decision removes.
++- **Carrying the profile path on the launch environment snapshot.** That snapshot is a process-environment projection with a single owner, and identity plus mutation semantics would sit on a value passed to every plugin that never writes a row.
++- **`loadOptionalPatches()` as the read path.** It is the fail-loud boot parser and anchors relative names at parse time, so writing its output back would persist `file:` URLs over `./probe.mjs`. Reading authored content needs the unanchored parser this change adds.
++- **Providing the service after boot settles.** Rows activate while the tree mounts; a row that asks for `profilePatches` after settlement would be a second ordering rule beside Cordis injection, and the field report's write-during-reload hazard is about the file, not about who provides the service.
++- **Making `plugin-inventory` writable** (`packages/host/plugin-inventory/README.md` keeps it read-only by design) or routing the write through `ctx.settings`. The settings document is a separate user file; neither mounts, unmounts, or hot-swaps a composition row.
++- **Letting the service own row semantics** (insert, enable, remove a named plugin). Row identity, module-name assertions, and `disabled` placement belong to the caller's patch edits; a service that knew them would freeze one row layout into `app-boot`.
++
++## Consequences
++
++- A management surface addresses the active profile layer without reconstructing `$DSH_HOME/profiles/<name>`, and its writes serialize against other writers and the launcher's watcher.
++- A live-reload profile applies a commit through the existing `watchUserPatches` path; a `startup`-frozen profile reports `startup` and applies at the next launch.
++- The service edits a patch list, not plugins: it does not resolve package names, does not validate that an inserted module exists, and keeps no backup generation. Those remain caller policy, and `validate` is the seam for them.
++- Only `@deepseek-ai/dsh-app-boot` gains public exports — the module, the service, the parser, and the types. `js-yaml` and `@deepseek-ai/dsh-atomic-write` are already dependencies of that package, so the launcher gains no new dependency.
++- `mutate` returns the committed list, but a watcher-applied generation arrives through `subscribe`; a listener must be idempotent because the watcher may publish one write more than once.
++- The profile layer is the only layer this service writes. The home layer stays operator-owned; a plugin that wants an edit to apply to every profile has no seam here.
++- `higherPrecedenceIds()` reads explicit `id` fields and inserted rows only; a claim expressed by a group or another patch verb stays invisible.
++- An out-of-tree manager on this line keeps working while the profile-layer service is absent, but it reimplements discovery and races the watcher — the state this decision removes once the release carries it.
